1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,007
Lesson 3.2.

2
00:00:02,387 --> 00:00:05,601
Entropy production and the
second law for trajectories.

3
00:00:06,717 --> 00:00:09,995
The second law says total
entropy does not decrease.

4
00:00:10,375 --> 00:00:13,391
In the previous lesson we
saw individual trajectories

5
00:00:13,391 --> 00:00:15,953
with negative work,
which seem to violate it.

6
00:00:16,333 --> 00:00:18,525
By how much do they
violate it, and how often?

7
00:00:18,905 --> 00:00:21,789
The answer is one of the
most beautiful laws of

8
00:00:21,789 --> 00:00:24,305
modern physics: the fluctuation theorems.

9
00:00:24,685 --> 00:00:27,879
They turn the inequality of
the second law into an exact

10
00:00:27,879 --> 00:00:30,960
equality about the distribution
of entropy production.

11
00:00:32,083 --> 00:00:35,996
Imagine filming a trajectory
and playing it backward.

12
00:00:36,376 --> 00:00:40,645
If the system is at equilibrium, the
reversed movie is just as plausible.

13
00:00:41,025 --> 00:00:44,514
If a force is pushing it,
the reversed movie is less

14
00:00:44,514 --> 00:00:47,599
likely: the particle
climbs against the force.

15
00:00:47,979 --> 00:00:52,494
Entropy production measures exactly
that: the log of the ratio between

16
00:00:52,494 --> 00:00:56,557
the probability of the trajectory
and that of its time reverse.

17
00:00:56,937 --> 00:00:58,924
It is a measure of irreversibility.

18
00:01:00,050 --> 00:01:05,228
The total entropy production along a
trajectory, in units of k_B, is the change

19
00:01:05,228 --> 00:01:09,620
in system entropy plus the heat
given to the bath divided by k_B T.

20
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,514
And it is exactly the log of the
ratio of probabilities of the

21
00:01:15,514 --> 00:01:20,406
forward trajectory and its
reverse: Σ = ln P[x]/P̃[x̃].

22
00:01:20,786 --> 00:01:25,239
In a steady state, its mean rate
is written with the probability

23
00:01:25,239 --> 00:01:29,762
current: the integral of J²/(D
p), which is positive and vanishes

24
00:01:29,762 --> 00:01:33,450
only if the current is zero,
that is, at equilibrium.

25
00:01:34,550 --> 00:01:40,233
An exact equality follows from that
definition: the average of e^(−Σ) is one.

26
00:01:40,613 --> 00:01:43,709
Always, for any protocol and any duration.

27
00:01:44,089 --> 00:01:47,868
Applying Jensen's inequality,
the average of Σ is greater

28
00:01:47,868 --> 00:01:51,514
than or equal to zero: the
second law is a consequence.

29
00:01:51,894 --> 00:01:55,630
But the equality says more: for
the average of the exponential

30
00:01:55,630 --> 00:01:58,884
to be one, there must be
trajectories with negative Σ.

31
00:01:59,264 --> 00:02:03,620
Individual violations are not
an accident: they are mandatory.

32
00:02:04,750 --> 00:02:10,984
In a steady state there is an even stronger
version: the probability of producing an

33
00:02:10,984 --> 00:02:16,475
amount Σ of entropy, divided by that
of producing minus Σ, is exactly e^Σ.

34
00:02:16,855 --> 00:02:21,220
Let's check it with a particle on
a ring pushed by a constant force.

35
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:24,333
The histogram of the
entropy produced in an

36
00:02:24,333 --> 00:02:26,748
interval is Gaussian and crosses zero.

37
00:02:27,128 --> 00:02:31,180
Plotting the log of the ratio
between the probability of Σ and

38
00:02:31,180 --> 00:02:34,970
that of minus Σ, the points
fall on the line of slope one.

39
00:02:35,350 --> 00:02:40,317
Trajectories that destroy entropy
exist, but they are exponentially rare.

40
00:02:41,450 --> 00:02:47,099
With mobility one, k_B T one, force
one half and a time window of two,

41
00:02:47,099 --> 00:02:52,103
entropy production is Gaussian
with mean 0.5 and variance one.

42
00:02:52,483 --> 00:02:58,116
The probability that a trajectory destroys
entropy is about thirty-one percent.

43
00:02:58,496 --> 00:03:02,948
If we lengthen the window to twenty,
the mean rises to five and that

44
00:03:02,948 --> 00:03:07,400
probability falls to about six
percent; at two hundred, to less than

45
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:11,001
one in a million: the second
law emerges at long times.

46
00:03:12,117 --> 00:03:15,035
Entropy production is
the irreducible energy

47
00:03:15,035 --> 00:03:17,489
cost of any irreversible computation.

48
00:03:17,869 --> 00:03:21,478
It governs the precision of
clocks and molecular motors, it

49
00:03:21,478 --> 00:03:24,966
can be inferred from measured
trajectories, and in a chip

50
00:03:24,966 --> 00:03:28,270
driven out of equilibrium
it is produced continuously.

51
00:03:29,383 --> 00:03:32,579
Common mistakes: thinking
a trajectory with negative

52
00:03:32,579 --> 00:03:36,081
production violates the second
law, when it is mandatory.

53
00:03:36,461 --> 00:03:39,338
Forgetting the system
entropy in transients.

54
00:03:39,718 --> 00:03:42,683
Applying the detailed theorem
outside its conditions.

55
00:03:43,063 --> 00:03:45,744
And confusing entropy
production with heat.

56
00:03:46,850 --> 00:03:51,110
In summary: the entropy production
of a trajectory is the log of the

57
00:03:51,110 --> 00:03:54,806
ratio between its probability
and that of its time reverse.

58
00:03:55,186 --> 00:03:58,371
It satisfies the equality ⟨e^(−Σ)⟩ = 1, of

59
00:03:58,371 --> 00:04:01,253
which the second law is a consequence.

60
00:04:01,633 --> 00:04:04,858
In a steady state, the
probability of destroying entropy

61
00:04:04,858 --> 00:04:07,796
is exponentially smaller
than that of producing it.

62
00:04:08,917 --> 00:04:12,673
To practice: deduce the second
law from the integral equality.

63
00:04:13,053 --> 00:04:17,689
Show that a Gaussian production must
have variance equal to twice the mean.

64
00:04:18,069 --> 00:04:20,328
Compute an entropy production rate.

65
00:04:20,708 --> 00:04:23,247
And evaluate a ratio from
the detailed theorem.
